import {{invokerPackage}}.Paginator;
import {{invokerPackage}}.{{clientClassName}};
{{#pagedOperation}}
{{#vendorExtensions.op.pagination.itemModel}}
import {{modelPackage}}.{{{.}}};
{{/vendorExtensions.op.pagination.itemModel}}
{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}
{{#isOptions}}
import {{apiPackage}}.options.{{{dataType}}};
{{/isOptions}}
{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}

class ListEverything {
    static void read({{clientClassName}} client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{{dataType}}} {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}) {
        {{{vendorExtensions.op.pagination.javaPaginatorType}}} list = client.{{#pagedGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/pagedGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.pagesMethodName}}({{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}{{^-first}}, {{/-first}}{{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}});
        for ({{{vendorExtensions.op.pagination.itemType}}} item : list) {
            System.out.println(item);
        }
        for (Page<{{{vendorExtensions.op.pagination.itemType}}}, {{#vendorExtensions.op.pagination.isToken}}{{{vendorExtensions.op.pagination.cursorType}}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.pagination.isToken}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.pagination.isLink}}Void{{/vendorExtensions.op.pagination.isLink}}> page : list.pages()) {
            System.out.println(page.items().size() + " items, status " + page.result().statusCode());
        }
    }
}
```
{{/pagedOperation}}
{{/hasPagedOperation}}

The cursor you pass to the method is the starting position, and the next cursor of a page resumes the list: pass it as the starting cursor of a new call. The page size and every other parameter, option and header are repeated unchanged on each page request, and each page is an ordinary call with its own retries, timeout, redirects, authentication and redaction. An error on a later page is raised from the read that needed it, after the items of the earlier pages were delivered, and ends the iteration; the SDK never skips a page. A list ends when the next cursor is absent, `null` or empty, and an empty page with a next cursor continues. A page that returns the cursor that was just sent raises `PaginationException`, a direct child of `{{errorPrefix}}Exception`, after its items were delivered, instead of looping. There is no total limit and no collect-all: use `Stream.limit`.

A list that is followed by a `Link` header (`rel="next"`, RFC 8288) takes the next URL from the header of each response. A next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationException` and nothing, no credentials either, is sent to it; such a method has no starting cursor, so it cannot resume.

The generator finds a paged operation by its names: a GET with one JSON 2xx response that has one array property, and either one cursor query parameter among `cursor`, `after`, `page_token`, `next_token`, `continuation_token` and `marker` paired with one next-cursor property among `next_cursor`, `next_page_token`, `next_token`, `next_marker`, `next_continuation_token`, `continuation_token` and `next` of the same type, or a `Link` header declared on the response. An operation that is ambiguous, or is streamed, keeps only its plain method, and the generation log names it and says why. The `x-fern-pagination` and `x-speakeasy-pagination` (cursor type) extensions are read as hints. The generator option `paginationOverrides` (a JSON object that maps an operation id to `none` or to `{style, requestParam, responseProperty, itemsProperty}`) wins over a hint and over inference, and `pagination=off` switches the feature off.

### Custom HTTP client

```java statements
import {{invokerPackage}}.TransportOptions;

var httpClient = java.net.http.HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .followRedirects(java.net.http.HttpClient.Redirect.NEVER)
    .build();
var transport = TransportOptions.builder().httpClient(httpClient).build();
```

The SDK sends every request through your client and never closes it. Combining it with `proxy`, `caCertPath` or `verifySsl(false)` fails at `build()`.

### Closing the client

`close()` is graceful and idempotent: it marks the client closed and returns, requests already in flight finish, and the transport the SDK created is released when the last of them completes. A request issued after that throws `IllegalStateException` ("ApiClient has been closed and can no longer send requests"). Streams that are still open are cancelled. A `java.net.http.HttpClient` you supplied is never closed.

### Cookie parameters

A cookie parameter is sent exactly as given when every character is a legal RFC 6265 cookie-octet (`%` is one, so a value you percent-encoded yourself goes out unchanged). Any other value (space, double quote, comma, semicolon, backslash, control or non-ASCII character) throws `IllegalArgumentException` before the request is sent; percent-encode it first. The SDK never encodes a cookie value.

### Cancellation

Interrupt the calling thread; the call throws `java.util.concurrent.CancellationException` (the `InterruptedException` is its cause) and is never reported as `NetworkTimeoutException`.

